    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A land of the Bohemian crown with its own estates, diet and law, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> from 1182 to 1918.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> sheltered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Unity of the Brethren</a>, who printed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kralice-bible">Kralice Bible</a>, and Anabaptist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterite</a> communities.</li><li>After 1620 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> curbed its estates; it ended as a crown land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1918.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The worldwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a> takes its name from Brethren refugees who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1722; its missionaries reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> by 1732.</p></aside>
    </section>

