    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa.jpg" alt="Frederick Barbarossa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_I._Barbarossa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Montecappio, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, the land along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morava-river">Morava</a> river between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Silesia, had been the core of Great Moravia in the ninth century. After that state's collapse it passed, probably in the early eleventh century, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid</a> dukes of Bohemia, who divided it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> principalities centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a> and granted them to junior members of the dynasty. The margraviate was born out of a dynastic quarrel. In 1182, after Bohemian nobles had tried to replace Duke Frederick with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-iii-otto">Conrad III Otto</a>, prince of Znojmo, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> restored Frederick to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> but made Conrad Otto margrave of Moravia, holding the land directly from the emperor. The arrangement did not last: Conrad Otto himself became duke of Bohemia in 1189, and once the Bohemian rulers had secured a hereditary royal title, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-sicily">Golden Bull of Sicily</a> in 1212, Moravia settled into its lasting position as a margraviate held under the Bohemian crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-iii-duke-of-bohemia">Vladislaus Henry</a>, brother of King Ottokar I, governed it as margrave from 1197 to 1222 and absorbed the old appanages, so that by the early thirteenth century the separate principalities had merged into a single land with its own nobility, courts and sense of identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/brno"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brno.jpg" alt="Brno" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brno_Montage_IV.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brno-Freiheitsplatz2.jpg: SchiDD Brno_Katedrála_sv._Petra_a_Pavla_(St.Peter_and_St.Paul_Cathedral).jpg: JRodSilva Brn..., CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From the thirteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> was transformed by colonisation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archdiocese-of-olomouc">Bishops of Olomouc</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-of-schauenburg">Bruno of Schauenburg</a> (1245–1281), monasteries and lay lords invited German-speaking settlers who cleared forest, founded villages under German law and populated new towns, while Czech-speaking peasants remained the majority in much of the countryside. Royal towns including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/znojmo">Znojmo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihlava">Jihlava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uherske-hradiste">Uherské Hradiště</a> received charters, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining made Jihlava one of the richest towns of the Czech lands. The economy rested on grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, cloth and the wine of the south around Znojmo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulov">Mikulov</a>. Religious life became unusually diverse after the Hussite era: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utraquism">Utraquists</a>, Catholics and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Unity of the Brethren</a> coexisted under noble protection, and in the sixteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterites</a> built communal settlements on Moravian estates. The Brethren printed the six-volume <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kralice-bible">Kralice Bible</a> between 1579 and 1593, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-amos-comenius">Jan Amos Comenius</a>, born in Moravia in 1592, became their best-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. After 1620 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> was restored by force, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> university of Olomouc, founded in 1573, as a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>. In the nineteenth century Brno's woollen mills earned it the name of the Moravian Manchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrava">Ostrava</a> grew around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitkovice-ironworks">Vítkovice ironworks</a>, and in Brno's Augustinian abbey <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Gregor Mendel</a> carried out the plant experiments he published in 1866.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld.jpg" alt="Battle on the Marchfeld" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle on the Marchfeld</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IV_L%C3%A1szl%C3%B3_%C3%A9s_Rudolf.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mór Than, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a land of the Bohemian crown, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> had no standing army of its own; its defence rested on noble levies summoned by the margrave or the diet, on royal castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spilberk-castle">Špilberk</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, and on walled towns. Lying on the routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, it was repeatedly a battleground. Mongol raiders crossed it in 1241, and in 1278 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a>, who had used Moravia as a base for his Austrian ambitions, was killed fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-i-of-germany">Rudolf of Habsburg</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-marchfeld">Battle on the Marchfeld</a>, just beyond the Moravian border. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a> of 1419–1434 most Moravian royal towns and many lords stayed loyal to Sigismund, and Hussite armies devastated parts of the land. From 1468 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus of Hungary</a> waged war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a> and then on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vladislaus II</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-olomouc">Peace of Olomouc</a> in 1479 left Moravia in Hungarian hands until Matthias died in 1490. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War Swedish forces held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a> from 1642 to 1650, but Brno withstood a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lennart-torstensson">Lennart Torstensson</a> in 1645. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>'s siege of Olomouc failed in 1758, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-austerlitz">Battle of Austerlitz</a> near Brno in 1805, and in 1866 Prussian troops occupied Brno before the preliminary peace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> was signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikulov">Mikulov</a> (Nikolsburg).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia.jpg" alt="John Henry, Margrave of Moravia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia">John Henry, Margrave of Moravia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busta_Jan_Jindrich_face.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Packare, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> was governed as a distinct land rather than a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The margrave, at first a junior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/premyslid-dynasty">Přemyslid</a>, later a Luxembourg prince and from 1526 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> king of Bohemia himself, ruled alongside the Moravian estates of lords, knights, prelates and royal towns, who met in the diet and kept their own land court and land tables, the registers of noble property and judgements, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>. Charles IV, margrave from 1334, declared Moravia a fief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lands-of-the-bohemian-crown">Crown of Bohemia</a> in 1348 and in 1349 granted it to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia">John Henry</a>, whose sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jobst-of-moravia">Jobst</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prokop-of-moravia">Prokop</a> fought each other over it for years; Jobst, who also held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1410 but died in 1411. The estates were strongest in the sixteenth and early seventeenth centuries. In 1608, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karel-the-elder-of-zerotin">Karel the Elder of Žerotín</a>, they sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-holy-roman-emperor">Archduke Matthias</a> against Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Rudolf II</a> and had their liberties confirmed. After the Bohemian Revolt failed, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renewed-land-ordinance">Renewed Land Ordinance</a> of 1628 made the crown hereditary in the Habsburg line and curbed the diet. Brno became the seat of the land offices in the 1640s; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s reforms from 1749 drew Moravia into a centralised Austrian administration. The diet revived after 1861, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-compromise">Moravian Compromise</a> of 1905 divided its seats between Czech and German electorates.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kralice-bible"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kralice-bible.jpg" alt="Kralice Bible" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kralice-bible">Kralice Bible</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kralice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>'s influence was out of proportion to its size. Its name kept alive the memory of Great Moravia and the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>, central to later Czech and Slovak national histories, and it gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Church</a>: Brethren refugees from Moravia settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> in 1722 under Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf">Nikolaus Ludwig von Zinzendorf</a>, and their renewed church sent missionaries to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kralice-bible">Kralice Bible</a> served as a model of literary Czech for centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-amos-comenius">Comenius</a>'s writings on education were read across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Moravia's sixteenth-century tolerance, though short-lived, sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptists</a> whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutterites">Hutterite</a> descendants live today in North America. In the nineteenth century the land produced a remarkable range of figures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Mendel</a>'s work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> became a foundation of genetics after its rediscovery in 1900; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-garrigue-masaryk">Tomáš Garrigue Masaryk</a>, first president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodonin">Hodonín</a> in 1850 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribor">Příbor</a> in 1856; and the composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leos-janacek">Leoš Janáček</a> made his career in Brno. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kremsier-parliament">Austrian Reichstag</a> of 1848–1849 sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kromeriz">Kroměříž</a>, where it drafted a federal constitution that was never put into force. Moravia's long coexistence of Czech and German speakers, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-compromise">Moravian Compromise</a> of 1905, were later discussed as a possible model for managing national conflict within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The margraviate did not collapse so much as dissolve into larger states. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> its separate institutions were steadily reduced: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renewed-land-ordinance">Renewed Land Ordinance</a> of 1628 subordinated the estates, the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Joseph II</a> centralised taxation and administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and from 1782 until the mid-nineteenth century Austrian Silesia was governed together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>. From 1804 Moravia was a crown land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and, after 1867, of the Austrian half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, with its own diet and governor. Rivalry between the Czech and German national movements dominated its politics, eased only in part by the Compromise of 1905. When Austria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> broke apart at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> was proclaimed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 28 October 1918 and Moravia became one of its lands; German-speaking districts in the north and south tried to join German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> but were occupied by Czechoslovak troops within weeks. In 1928 Moravia was merged with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-silesia">Czech Silesia</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-silesian-land">Moravian-Silesian Land</a>, which the communist regime dissolved in 1949 when it replaced the historic lands with regions. Most of Moravia's German speakers were expelled in 1945–1946. Moravia survives as a historical land forming the eastern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, with Brno as its largest city and a strong regional identity.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> kept its own estates, diet and law as a margraviate of the Bohemian crown for more than seven centuries, shaped Czech religious and literary history through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Unity of the Brethren</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kralice-bible">Kralice Bible</a>, and produced figures from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-amos-comenius">Comenius</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregor-mendel">Mendel</a> before it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> in 1918.</em></p>

